# Workspace grouping-directory .envrc
#
# Every directory in the git workspace tree that groups repositories (the
# workspace root, each visibility, owner and family directory) has an .envrc
# that is a symlink to this one file. It is installed by home-manager from
# nix-home (modules/home-manager/direnv/workspace.envrc): change it there,
# never through a symlink or a copy.
#
# Background
#
# Repositories sit several levels below the workspace root. Each repository's
# own .envrc sets up its dev shell: a nix flake with its tools, exported
# variables, and helper settings. A shell or AI agent session started in a
# directory above the repositories used to get none of that, so a "generic"
# session in a family directory could not run the tools any of its
# repositories need.
#
# What this does
#
# `use children` (a direnv stdlib function, also from nix-home) sources the
# .envrc of every immediate child directory. Grouping directories below this
# one run this same file, so starting at any level loads every repository
# beneath it. It also writes .mcp.json and .claude/settings.json here, merged
# from the children's MCP servers and enabled plugins, so an agent session
# started here sees them too.
#
# A repository whose .envrc calls source_up lands back in this file. There
# `use children` does nothing: a directory gathers its children only when it
# is the directory being loaded or sits beneath it, so entering one
# repository never loads its siblings.
use children

# Per-directory additions that belong to this directory only (for example a
# dev shell every child shares). Not version controlled; keep it next to the
# symlink.
source_env_if_exists .envrc.local
